<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head>
    <title><%= content_for(:title) || "Assistant" %></title>
    <%# Two independent hints, both about the bottom of a phone.

        viewport-fit=cover lets the page paint into the notch and
        home-indicator margins, which is what makes env(safe-area-inset-*)
        report anything but zero — the nav's bottom toggle offsets by it, and
        so does the chat composer.

        interactive-widget=resizes-content keeps a bottom-welded composer above
        the on-screen keyboard on Chrome for Android: the default
        (resizes-visual) shrinks only the visual viewport, so anything pinned
        to the bottom of the layout ends up *behind* the keyboard. iOS Safari
        ignores the hint and needs visualViewport measurement instead — see
        chat_viewport_controller. %>
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1,viewport-fit=cover,interactive-widget=resizes-content">
    <meta name="apple-mobile-web-app-capable" content="yes">
    <meta name="apple-mobile-web-app-title" content="Assistant">
    <meta name="application-name" content="Assistant">
    <meta name="mobile-web-app-capable" content="yes">
    <%# The surface tone, so the phone's status bar continues the header
        rather than sitting on a white band above it. %>
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#ebddc5">
    <%# `default` keeps iOS's status bar *outside* the web view when installed,
        which is what makes `100dvh` still mean the area the app actually has —
        the chat screen (D-chat-shell) sizes itself against exactly that.
        black-translucent would look better and would need a top safe-area
        inset on every page: a separate change. %>
    <meta name="apple-mobile-web-app-status-bar-style" content="default">
    <%# Turbo page refreshes — a redirect back to the URL you're already on,
        and every broadcast_refresh_to — morph the DOM in place and keep the
        scroll where it was, instead of re-rendering the page and throwing
        the reader back to the top. The chat surface is what makes this
        visible (sending a message and receiving a reply are both refreshes),
        but every redirect-to-self in the app gets the same smoothness, and
        broadcast_refresh_to has assumed morphing since Turbo 8. %>
    <meta name="turbo-refresh-method" content="morph">
    <meta name="turbo-refresh-scroll" content="preserve">
    <%= csrf_meta_tags %>
    <%= csp_meta_tag %>

    <%= yield :head %>

    <link rel="icon" href="/icon.png" type="image/png">
    <link rel="icon" href="/icon.svg" type="image/svg+xml">
    <link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="/icon.png">
    <%# Installable. An installed copy also gets the left screen edge back
        from the browser's own back gesture, which is what makes the nav's
        edge swipe work in full — but the button stays regardless, because a
        tab in a browser is still the common case. %>
    <%= tag.link rel: "manifest", href: pwa_manifest_path %>

    <%# The @font-face rules first (Propshaft digests their url()s), then
        the Tailwind build that consumes the families. %>
    <%= stylesheet_link_tag "application", "data-turbo-track": "reload" %>
    <%= stylesheet_link_tag :app, "data-turbo-track": "reload" %>
    <%= javascript_importmap_tags %>
  </head>

  <%# On `<body>` because it is the one element a morph keeps: Turbo morphs
      body's *children*, so a controller here survives the render it is
      bridging (see focus_keeper_controller). %>
  <body class="bg-canvas text-ink antialiased"
        data-controller="focus-keeper"
        data-action="turbo:submit-start@document->focus-keeper#remember
                     turbo:render@document->focus-keeper#restore">
    <% if authenticated? %>
      <%= render "shared/celebration" %>
      <div data-controller="nav"
           data-action="keydown.esc@window->nav#close turbo:before-visit@window->nav#close
                        touchstart->nav#swipeStart touchmove->nav#swipeMove touchend->nav#swipeEnd touchcancel->nav#swipeEnd">
        <%# The height is a token rather than padding because a bleed page has
            to subtract it — see --app-header-h. 3.5rem is the 56px the
            py-1.5 around a size-11 button already came to. %>
        <header class="sticky top-0 z-20 flex h-[var(--app-header-h)] items-center gap-1 bg-surface px-2 lg:hidden print:hidden">
          <button type="button" class="flex size-11 cursor-pointer items-center justify-center rounded-lg text-soft hover:bg-stone-100 hover:text-ink"
                  data-action="nav#toggle" aria-label="Open menu">
            <%= icon(:bars_3, css_class: "size-6") %>
          </button>
          <%= link_to root_path, class: "flex items-center gap-2 px-2 py-2" do %>
            <span class="size-2.5 rounded-full bg-primary-600"></span>
            <span class="display text-lg">Assistant</span>
          <% end %>
        </header>

        <div class="hidden fixed inset-0 z-30 bg-black/40 lg:hidden" data-nav-target="backdrop" data-action="click->nav#close"></div>

        <%# The same toggle as the header's, in the half of the screen a thumb
            can actually reach. The header hamburger is where the eye looks
            for a menu, so it stays; on a 6-inch phone it is also the one
            corner the hand holding the phone cannot get to without
            regripping, and that is the whole complaint this answers.
            Bottom-left because that is the edge the drawer arrives from, and
            because the bottom-right slot belongs to a primary action.
            z-20 puts it under the backdrop and the drawer, so an open drawer
            simply covers it — no second state to keep in sync.

            Not on a bleed page: there the bottom-left corner is the chat
            composer (D-chat-shell), and a floating button would land on top
            of it. Those pages carry their own back affordance in the screen
            bar, and the header hamburger is still where it always was. %>
        <% unless content_for?(:bleed) %>
          <button type="button" data-action="nav#toggle" aria-label="Open menu"
                  class="fixed bottom-[calc(1rem_+_env(safe-area-inset-bottom))] left-4 z-20 flex size-12 cursor-pointer items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-surface/95 text-ink shadow-lg ring-1 ring-black/10 backdrop-blur-sm active:bg-stone-200 lg:hidden print:hidden">
            <%= icon(:bars_3, css_class: "size-6") %>
          </button>
        <% end %>

        <%# A bleed page is exactly the viewport minus the header above it, and
            hides its own overflow: otherwise `min-h-screen` stacks a full
            viewport *under* the header and the document scrolls by the height
            of the header — which is the second scroller the chat screen exists
            to remove. %>
        <div class="lg:flex <%= content_for?(:bleed) ? "h-[calc(100dvh-var(--app-header-h))] overflow-hidden" : "min-h-screen" %>">
        <%# The rail. `nav-rail` + data-nav-collapsed drive the desktop
            icon-only mode from one attribute (see application.css) so the
            labels don't each need a class toggled onto them. %>
        <aside class="nav-rail print:hidden fixed inset-y-0 left-0 z-40 flex h-dvh w-64 shrink-0 -translate-x-full flex-col bg-surface transition-transform duration-200 lg:sticky lg:top-0 lg:translate-x-0 lg:transition-[width] lg:duration-200 lg:data-[nav-collapsed=true]:w-[68px]"
               data-nav-target="drawer" data-nav-collapsed="false">
          <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-1 pr-2 lg:pr-3">
            <%= link_to root_path, class: "flex min-w-0 items-center gap-2 px-6 py-5 lg:px-5" do %>
              <span class="size-2.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-primary-600"></span>
              <span class="nav-label display text-lg">Assistant</span>
            <% end %>
            <button type="button" class="flex size-11 cursor-pointer items-center justify-center rounded-lg text-soft hover:bg-stone-100 hover:text-ink lg:hidden"
                    data-action="nav#close" aria-label="Close menu">
              <%= icon(:x_mark, css_class: "size-6") %>
            </button>
            <%# Desktop-only: collapsing an off-canvas drawer to unlabelled
                icons would be a worse drawer, so this is hidden below lg. %>
            <button type="button" class="hidden size-8 shrink-0 cursor-pointer items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-canvas text-soft hover:bg-stone-200 hover:text-ink lg:flex"
                    data-action="nav#toggleCollapsed" aria-label="Collapse or expand the sidebar" title="Collapse or expand the sidebar">
              <%= icon(:chevrons_left_right, css_class: "size-4") %>
            </button>
          </div>

          <nav class="flex-1 space-y-6 overflow-y-auto px-3 pb-4">
            <div class="space-y-0.5">
              <%= nav_link_to "Dashboard", root_path, :home %>
              <%= nav_link_to "Today", today_path, :inbox %>
              <%= nav_link_to "Calendar", calendar_path, :calendar %>
              <%= nav_link_to "Tasks", tasks_path, :check_circle %>
              <%= nav_link_to "Conversations", chats_path, :chat_bubble %>
            </div>

            <div>
              <p class="nav-label mb-1 px-3 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.12em] text-stone-600">Your life</p>
              <div class="space-y-0.5">
                <% Current.user.enabled_sections.each do |section| %>
                  <%= section_nav_link(section) %>
                <% end %>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div>
              <p class="nav-label mb-1 px-3 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.12em] text-stone-600">Vault</p>
              <div class="space-y-0.5">
                <%= nav_link_to "Address Book", people_path, :users %>
                <%= nav_link_to "Photos", photos_path, :photo %>
                <%= nav_link_to "Recipes", recipes_path, :book_open %>
                <%= nav_link_to "Documents", documents_path, :document %>
                <%= nav_link_to "Notes", notes_path, :note %>
                <% if Current.user.linked_accounts.where(provider: "email").exists? %>
                  <%= nav_link_to "Mail", mail_index_path, :envelope %>
                <% end %>
                <%= nav_link_to "Passwords", credentials_path, :key %>
              </div>
            </div>
          </nav>

          <%# The three settings destinations fold into a menu above the user
              button (design 2a), so the rail's foot is one row instead of
              four. Sign out lives in the menu too — it was the one thing
              here that could be hit by accident. %>
          <div class="relative p-3" data-controller="popover"
               data-action="keydown.esc@window->popover#close click@window->popover#clickOutside">
            <div class="hidden absolute inset-x-3 bottom-full mb-1.5 flex-col gap-0.5 rounded-2xl bg-canvas p-1.5 shadow-lg"
                 data-popover-target="panel" role="menu">
              <%= link_to settings_path, class: "flex items-center gap-2.5 whitespace-nowrap rounded-full px-3 py-2 text-sm text-ink hover:bg-surface", role: "menuitem" do %>
                <%= icon(:cog, css_class: "size-4 shrink-0") %>Personal settings
              <% end %>
              <%= link_to household_path, class: "flex items-center gap-2.5 whitespace-nowrap rounded-full px-3 py-2 text-sm text-ink hover:bg-surface", role: "menuitem" do %>
                <%= icon(:home_modern, css_class: "size-4 shrink-0") %>Household settings
              <% end %>
              <% if Current.user.admin? %>
                <%= link_to admin_root_path, class: "flex items-center gap-2.5 whitespace-nowrap rounded-full px-3 py-2 text-sm text-ink hover:bg-surface", role: "menuitem" do %>
                  <%= icon(:warning, css_class: "size-4 shrink-0") %>Admin settings
                <% end %>
              <% end %>
              <%= button_to "Sign out", session_path, method: :delete, role: "menuitem",
                    class: "w-full cursor-pointer rounded-full px-3 py-2 text-left text-sm text-soft hover:bg-surface hover:text-ink" %>
            </div>

            <button type="button" data-action="popover#toggle" data-popover-target="trigger"
                    aria-haspopup="menu" aria-expanded="false"
                    class="flex w-full cursor-pointer items-center gap-2 rounded-full bg-canvas px-2 py-1.5 text-left hover:bg-stone-200">
              <%= render Ui::AvatarComponent.new(user: Current.user, size: :sm) %>
              <span class="nav-label min-w-0 flex-1 truncate text-sm font-semibold text-ink"><%= Current.user.name %></span>
              <%= icon(:cog, css_class: "nav-label size-4 shrink-0 text-soft") %>
            </button>
          </div>
        </aside>

        <%# A `bleed` page owns the viewport instead of flowing inside it:
            no page padding, no document scroll, so the one scroller it
            contains is the only thing that moves. The chat screen
            (D-chat-shell) is the only page that asks for this. %>
        <main class="min-w-0 flex-1">
          <% if content_for?(:bleed) %>
            <%= render Ui::FlashComponent.new(flash: flash) %>
            <%= yield %>
          <% else %>
            <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 py-6 sm:px-8 sm:py-8">
              <%= render Ui::FlashComponent.new(flash: flash) %>
              <%= yield %>
            </div>
          <% end %>
        </main>
        </div>
      </div>
    <% else %>
      <main class="flex min-h-screen items-center justify-center p-6">
        <div class="w-full max-w-sm">
          <div class="mb-6 flex items-center justify-center gap-2">
            <span class="size-2.5 rounded-full bg-primary-600"></span>
            <span class="display text-xl">Assistant</span>
          </div>
          <%= render Ui::FlashComponent.new(flash: flash) %>
          <%= yield %>
        </div>
      </main>
    <% end %>
  </body>
</html>
